Filed by the coordinator from lane-b060-b062's investigation, 2026-08-31.
The evidence below is the lane's; the notable feature is that the coredump
REFUTED the lane's own first hypothesis rather than confirming it.

## Measured

- `multi_root_tests` SIGSEGV'd 3 times consecutively on 2026-08-31 ~04:07-04:09,
  then passed 10/10 immediately after with identical code, then 0 failures in
  25 interleaved runs of the merge-base and branch binaries.
- Crash signature `SIGSEGV si_code=SEGV_ACCERR` - invalid permissions on a
  mapped page, i.e. a guard-page hit, i.e. a genuine stack overflow.
- **The stack is SHALLOW**: ~32 frames stored (systemd's cap), ~40 in an
  earlier live gdb read, bottoming out in tokio `Runtime::block_on`.
- Frame #0 is `toml_edit::parser::prelude::new_input`. Frames #10-#14 are
  `openplanet_lsp::config::read_config_file` (`src/config.rs:740`) ->
  `LspConfig::apply_config_layers` -> `load_with_parent` -> `load` ->
  `server::Backend::new`. Frame #18 is the async body of
  `multi_root_isolation_sibling_symbols_do_not_resolve`
  (`tests/multi_root_tests.rs:79`).
- The crashing thread is the tokio worker (TID 109672), not the main test
  thread (109671).
- **When a test binary crashes, `cargo test` LOWERS the suite total instead of
  reporting a failure.** Observed 2190 and 2218 against a true 2223.

## Inferred

Stack exhaustion on the tokio worker BEFORE the TOML parse begins. The large
`Backend::new` / `LspConfig` values inside a large async state machine are the
plausible consumer. This also explains the intermittency better than any
pressure story: if the frame sits right at the boundary, small variations in
what is on the stack tip it over, and nothing external needs to change.

## REFUTED: deep TOML recursion

The first hypothesis - "a stack overflow in toml_edit's recursive parser,
caused by deeply nested input" - is refuted by the coredump, and it is worth
recording HOW, because the reasoning is reusable.

A recursive-descent parser that overflows on nested input shows **hundreds or
thousands** of frames. This shows **tens**. And frame #0 is `new_input`, the
parser's ENTRY POINT, before any recursion has occurred - the crash happens on
the first call, in its own prologue. So the thread was already out of stack
when it arrived. **The TOML input is a bystander, not the cause.**

The generalisable form: when a stack overflow is blamed on recursion, the
stack DEPTH is the discriminator, and it is cheap to read. A shallow stack
means something consumed the stack before the suspect ran.

## UNSUPPORTED - do not act on: memory pressure

`available` was 21-22G throughout. The "~2G free" figure that circulated came
from reading the `free` column, which is the wrong one. `/tmp` was at 87% at
the time but has since been cleared to 18% (see B065), so neither pressure
explanation has anything left to stand on. **A fix that adds RAM or disk is
fixing the wrong thing.**

## Why this matters beyond one flake

It defeats **count reconciliation**, which is this batch's mandated
countermeasure for "are my tests actually running". A suite total that does
not reconcile now has two causes - tests not running, or a binary crashed -
and chasing the first when it was the second wastes the investigation. See
`docs/verification-hazards.md`.

## Suggested next step

Raise the tokio worker stack size for these tests, or box the large values in
`Backend::new`, and see whether it stops reproducing. Alternatively instrument
remaining stack at `LspConfig::load` under `#[tokio::test]`.

Reproduction is intermittent, so **establish determinism before bisecting**:
this same flake already let a bisect terminate with a confident wrong answer
(disable half - passes; re-enable partially - passes; restore in full -
passes), because each step was a single run of a flaky test.

## Evidence

`/home/xertrov/.cache/lsp-openplanet-builds/b060-flake-evidence/multi_root_tests-109671.core`
(3.6MB). NOTE: the matching binary has since been rebuilt, so gdb can no
longer read the core against it; the systemd-stored trace
(`coredumpctl info 109671`, while the boot lasts) is the durable artefact.

## A load correlate, observed 2026-08-31 09:00-09:20 (coordinator)

lane-b064 reported **load average 87** during its B072 gate — tests that
normally finish in 0.1s took 54s. I confirmed the tail of it: 15-minute
average 27.77 while the 1-minute had already fallen to 8.70, so the spike was
real and transient.

**Cause: aggregate oversubscription, not a violated limit.** Every lane runs
`cargo test -j2 -- --test-threads=2` as instructed. With five lanes plus their
codex reviewers each building concurrently, the machine sees 20+ compile and
test threads. Each lane is individually compliant; the sum is not.

**Why this belongs on THIS ticket.** The crash is a stack overflow on a tokio
worker whose stack was already exhausted before `toml_edit` was entered (see
the coredump analysis above). Scheduling pressure is a far better fit for the
observed intermittency - three crashes in a row at 04:07-04:09, then 10/10
green immediately after with identical code - than either of the two refuted
explanations:

- **memory pressure**: refuted, `available` was 21-22G throughout.
- **disk**: refuted, `/tmp` was cleared to 18% and the flake predates and
  postdates that.

Both refuted causes were *environmental* guesses that happened to be the wrong
environment. Load is the environmental variable that was actually moving, and
nobody was watching it. **This is not a confirmed cause** - it is a correlate
with a plausible mechanism (a deeper async state machine at the stack boundary
tips over more readily when the worker is descheduled mid-frame), and it is
recorded so the next investigator checks it rather than re-deriving the two
dead ends.

**Cheap discriminating test, before any fix:** try to reproduce under
deliberate load (`stress-ng` or several concurrent suite runs) versus on an
idle machine. If the crash rate moves with load, that is the strongest signal
available and it costs one afternoon of nobody else using the box. If it does
not move, load joins memory and disk on the refuted list and the remaining
hypothesis is purely the frame size.

**Operational note for whoever tests this:** a timing-sensitive result taken on
this machine today is untrustworthy. Check `uptime` before believing any
measurement whose meaning depends on duration.

## Coordinator correction (2026-08-31): the title's stated cause is REFUTED

This ticket's title and body say 'stack overflow at Backend::new to
LspConfig::load'. That was my diagnosis and it is wrong. I read
`si_code=SEGV_ACCERR` as a guard-page hit — its common cause — and never
checked `si_addr` against `rsp`.

lane-b064 measured the coredump directly:

- `rsp` sits about 2016 KiB above the bottom of a 2052 KiB stack mapping, so
  roughly **36 KiB of stack was in use**. Nothing was near a guard page.
- `si_addr == rip` exactly. That is an **execute** fault at the instruction
  pointer, not a data access that ran off the stack.
- The faulting address is at an **identical module offset across five different
  ASLR bases**, so it is deterministic in the module, not a function of where
  the allocator or the stack happened to land.

Three causes have now been proposed for this crash and all three are refuted —
each by a measurement nobody had taken at the time. Do not propose a fourth
without the measurement that distinguishes it.

The shallow stack also refutes the recursion theory recorded earlier, so the
`Backend::new` -> `LspConfig::load` path named in the title is not implicated
at all beyond being where the thread happened to be.

**Open question for whoever takes this**: identify what code lives at module
offset `+0x11f7320`. A deterministic execute fault at a fixed offset points at
something like a jump into unmapped or non-executable memory — a stale function
pointer, a torn vtable, or an unloaded/remapped code region — rather than
anything about stack depth.

The second half of the title stands and is independent of the cause: **a
crashed test binary silently lowers the suite total**, so the count must be
reconciled rather than assumed. That is the part worth fixing first, because it
makes every later run honest regardless of what the SIGSEGV turns out to be.

`docs/verification-hazards.md` has been marked accordingly (master 0b47535)
rather than rewritten to a fourth confident cause.

## RESOLVED (2026-08-31, lane-b064): the SIGSEGV is not a defect in this project

Measured from five preserved coredumps, reproducible from the cores alone via
`parse_core.py` / `readmem.py`. Two independent proofs that stack exhaustion is
refuted, neither needing the binary:

- **`si_addr == rip` exactly, in all five cores.** A stack access faults at a
  stack address. This faults at the instruction pointer, so it is an EXECUTE
  fault.
- **36 KiB of a 2052 KiB stack in use** — `rsp` is 0x9178 below the region top.
  98% of the stack was untouched.

Corroborated by a bisection run before the cores: the config-load path needs
between 44 and 48 KiB (survives 48, dies at 44, one process per size). Against
2052 KiB that is a 42x margin, consistent with the 36 KiB actually in use.

**`SEGV_ACCERR` does not mean "guard page".** It means mapped-but-wrong-
permission; the guard page is merely its most common cause. That inference was
mine and it was wrong.

### The fault is at a legitimate call target

The `call` executed correctly — the pushed return address was read off the
dumped stack in all five cores:

```
rip   = +0x11f7320   toml_edit::parser::prelude::new_input
[rsp] = +0x115f1ae   parse_document   <- exactly frame #1, all 5 cores
```

Control arrived at `new_input` legitimately: no corrupted function pointer, no
smashed return address, no bogus jump target. `rip` is the correct entry
address and its page is in a valid R-X mapping, verified from the core's own
PT_LOAD flags rather than from a rebuilt binary. **A present, executable page
cannot raise a permission fault on an instruction fetch** — not in this
program, not in any program.

Not test-specific: three different tests crashed at the identical offset
(`multi_root_stra` x2, `multi_root_per_` x2, `multi_root_isol`), across five
ASLR bases.

### Six further mechanisms, each refuted by measurement

| mechanism | refuted by |
|---|---|
| NUMA-balancing PTE trapping | `numa_balancing=0`, single node |
| THP file-backed collapse | THP is `[madvise]`, not `always` |
| CET shadow stack / IBT | would raise `SEGV_CPERR` (10); this is `ACCERR` (2) |
| binary rewritten under the running process | `ETXTBSY` — tested, not reasoned: the kernel refuses to open a running executable for writing |
| load | 40/40 clean at load 26.85 |
| hardware error | no MCE, no EDAC events in the journal, which does cover 04:07 |

### What is NOT established, stated as such

The positive cause. What exists is a fingerprint: one file, one offset, a
70-second window (04:07:43-04:08:53) then nothing in the following nine hours,
three different tests, and no other process on the machine faulting in that
window. That is the shape of a transiently bad page-cache page or PTE for one
text page of one executable, clearing when the page was later evicted and
re-read. It CANNOT be confirmed here: `/sys/devices/system/edac/mc/` has no
memory controller registered, so ECC errors are not reported on this box.

Recorded as the shape of the evidence, not as a diagnosis. **Four causes have
now been proposed for this crash and three were refuted by measurement; the
fourth is deliberately left unasserted.**

### Decision: do not "fix" the SIGSEGV

A bigger stack, a recursion limit, or a retry would each address nothing. The
exact binary is gone (core build-id `7a4a22be...`, the file at that path is now
`d9ea0e55...`, rebuilt 5h22m later; all 11 `multi_root_tests` binaries across
every build cache were scanned and none match), so `+0x11f7320` cannot be
disassembled — but that does not matter, because the fault is architecturally
impossible in software and there is nothing in our code to change.

## The actionable residue, and the reason this ticket stays open

**A crashed test binary silently lowers the suite total.** Measured: on a
crashed binary `cargo test` exits **101** and emits **ZERO** `test result`
lines. Cargo does not hide the crash — but any gate that sums
`grep "^test result"` drops those tests while showing no failure at all.

That is not hypothetical: it is what produced lane-b060-b062's reported
2190/2218 against a true 2223.

Fix: **check the exit code, and assert the expected binary count.** A total is
only trustworthy alongside proof that every binary reported one. This is worth
more than any conclusion about the crash, because it makes every future run
honest regardless of what else goes wrong in it.